  ✓ CONFIRMED: Manipulable Curve spot quote directly controls NonUST share 
    Justification: Upon reviewing Vault.totalUnderlying, _createDeposit, 
_createClaim, _withdrawDeposit, and NonUSTStrategy.investedAssets, I confirmed 
the price-manipulation vulnerability. NonUSTStrategy.investedAssets values the 
strategy’s UST-denominated assets using curvePool.get_dy_underlying(ustI, 
underlyingI, ustAssets), which is a live Curve spot quote with no TWAP, 
deviation, freshness, liquidity, or intra-transaction consistency check. 
Vault.totalUnderlying incorporates this quote directly, and _createClaim 
computes shares as amount * totalShares / totalUnderlyingMinusSponsored before 
the deposit transfer occurs. A temporary reduction in the Curve quote therefore 
lowers the denominator and mints excess shares to a depositor. After the pool is
restored, _withdrawDeposit recalculates the shares attributable to the recorded 
principal using the restored valuation, potentially burning fewer shares than 
were minted; the residual shares remain associated with the claimer and can be 
realized through yieldFor and claimYield, subject to available vault liquidity 
and the configured lock period. No code-level guard prevents this sequence, so 
the hypothesis is exploitable where the vault has existing shares and sufficient
liquidity.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Zero-output protection allows Curve price manipulation to st
    Justification: Upon reviewing NonUSTStrategy._swapUnderlyingToUst, 
_swapUstToUnderlying, and finishRedeemStable, I confirmed the stated 
price-manipulation vulnerability. Both swap paths call 
curvePool.exchange_underlying with min_dy set to zero, so Curve provides no 
execution-price protection: any non-reverting output amount is accepted. The 
redeem path is especially exploitable because BaseStrategy.finishRedeemStable is
public and NonUSTStrategy.finishRedeemStable invokes _swapUstToUnderlying 
immediately after receiving UST, allowing any caller to trigger settlement while
the pool is temporarily distorted. Although doHardWork is restricted, that 
restriction does not protect the redeem-side conversion. No trusted quote, 
oracle comparison, maximum price impact, liquidity check, or caller-supplied 
slippage bound is used before either swap. The investedAssets view uses 
get_dy_underlying, but this is only an informational valuation and does not 
constrain exchange_underlying execution. Consequently, an attacker able to 
manipulate the Curve pool around settlement can cause strategy UST or underlying
assets to be converted at an unfavorable rate; the zero minimum output permits 
the loss to execute rather than reverting.


